   {:report
    "The two documents are versions of the microFIT Contract (Version 2.0 vs Version 3.1.1). Overall the structure and most contractual provisions remain the same, but Version 3.1.1 introduces several procedural/administrative changes and removes the detailed Domestic Content requirements that were present in Version 2.0. Key changes (summarized here and detailed in the JSON outputs) are:\n\n- Removal of the Domestic Content Requirements and the Domestic Content Grids (Appendix C) and removal of the clause in the representations/warranties requiring compliance with the Minimum Required Domestic Content Level.\n- Introduction of a “Prescribed Form” concept (new defined term) and multiple procedural changes requiring actions, notices or forms to be made “in the Prescribed Form” (e.g., notification of changes in A(11), change of contact information, termination on notice, amendment).\n- Addition of a “Website” definition and expanded discretion for OPA (e.g., Project Eligibility exception subject to OPA consent; OPA discretion in deeming Existing Building).\n- Modifications to the Collection/Disclosure of Information language (adds explicit public health / emergency planning disclosure language and removes explicit reference to disclosure regarding Domestic Content compliance).\n- Minor renumbering/relabelling of clauses in Section 4.4 due to removal of the Domestic Content clause.\n- Clarifying edits in a number of notice/amendment/termination provisions (inserting Prescribed Form), and small expansions on OPA’s discretion and purposes for sharing information.\n\nEffect and recommendations:\n- The removal of Domestic Content provisions is a substantive policy change: suppliers reliant on those rules should be made aware (impact on eligibility or pricing conditions if their application depended on those rules).\n- The Prescribed Form requirement centralizes and standardizes how Suppliers must provide certain notices/requests. Parties should confirm where to find the Prescribed Form (the new “Website” definition points to the OPA website) and update internal procedures to comply.\n- The changes to disclosure (public health/emergency planning) broaden where facility/location information may be shared; Suppliers should review and, if necessary, seek clarification on what will be published/shared and the timing.\n- The addition of OPA discretion for consenting to otherwise excluded contracted facilities may permit exceptions; Suppliers should note this and consider seeking written confirmations where relevant.\n\nDetailed per-section diffs and concise key differences follow.",
